Rural credit unions are doing well where they exist. The problem is where they do not. Half of rural America’s counties have no credit union office, and most never did. Nearly half of all rural residents — about 11.6 million people — live more than ten miles from one.
Two things turn that from a statistic into a finding. First, the places without an office are often the places where the alternatives fail as well: the lowest broadband, the fewest cars, the oldest residents, the highest poverty. Four independent measures point to the same regions — Appalachia, the Mississippi Delta, and parts of Texas. Second, most of the people affected do not live in empty country. More than a million live in rural counties with real towns and are still far from any credit union.
The sharpest single fact is agricultural. Three quarters of the rural counties whose economies depend on farming have no credit union office at all.
One caveat governs every map. They show credit unions only; bank branches are not yet drawn. Where a bank stands on the corner, the gap is smaller than it appears here. Where none does, it is exactly as shown. Adding the bank layer is the next step, and it will make these maps directly comparable with the parallel bank-regulator study.

The maps, one line each
Where credit unions are, and where they are not
1 · Where they are.  461 rural credit unions, headquartered in only 321 of the 1,556 rural counties. They cluster in the Upper Midwest, the Northern Plains and Texas, and are almost absent from the interior West.
2 · How thinly they are spread.  The northern tier is well served. The Great Plains, the Deep South, Appalachia and Alaska mostly have no office at all.
3 · Where the people without an office live.  751 counties, 7.5 million people. Not the Plains — those counties are empty of people as well as offices. The Southeast, Appalachia and eastern Texas.
4 · One merger away.  461 rural counties are served by exactly one credit union, and in 352 of them that institution is based in another county.
Where the alternatives to a branch fail too
5 · Broadband.  306 counties have no office and the lowest broadband subscription — 3.0 million people for whom “bank online instead” is not an available answer.
6 · Age.  259 counties are among the oldest and have no office — 2.0 million people, the group least likely to move to digital banking.
7 · Cars.  249 counties have no office and the most households without a vehicle — 2.9 million people who cannot simply drive to the next county.
8 · Poverty.  259 counties have no office and the highest poverty — 3.1 million people. The Delta, the Black Belt and central Appalachia, almost exactly.
Agriculture and the moving definition
9 · Farming counties.  Of 393 farming-dependent rural counties, 294 have no credit union office. Three in four.
10 · The moving definition.  109 counties stopped counting as rural between the 2013 and 2024 classifications, and 48 credit unions went with them. The category the statute defines shrinks on its own.
Distance, and who is affected
11 · How far.  In 459 rural counties the average resident is more than 20 miles from a credit union office — 3.9 million people.
12 · The Federal Reserve’s line.  11.6 million rural residents live more than 10 miles from a credit union office, the threshold the Federal Reserve uses for rural banking deserts. In 885 counties, that is most of the county.
13 · Not explained by emptiness.  62 rural counties are both far from an office and comparatively densely settled — 1.3 million people in towns, not open country. Eastern Kentucky, the Delta, central Texas.
14 · Where those people are.  The same 11.6 million drawn as circles sized by headcount. The weight sits in the Southeast, Appalachia and Texas, not in the wide, empty counties of the West.

Three things to take away
It is a coverage problem, not a performance problem. The credit unions that exist are growing and earning. What is missing is presence in half the rural map.
The gap is concentrated, and it is in settled places. Appalachia, the Delta and Texas appear on map after map. These are not empty counties; they are towns without a credit union.
Extending existing credit unions is the realistic route. Sixty-eight rural counties gained their first office over the past decade, every one from an existing institution reaching further. Almost none came from a new charter. A branch delivers services; only a charter delivers local ownership — the report should price both.


 